WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.640
George Orwell was born
as Eric Blair in 1903.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.620
The name George Orwell -
his pen name -

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.440
was something that was invented to
be as English sounding as possible.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.040
Orwell is a river that
runs through Suffolk,

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.040
and George is that
quintessentially English name.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.180
So Orwell is kind of a created self.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.900
He is a version of himself that he
puts across in all of his writing -

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:46.720
in his essays, in his journalism.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.740
In a way, the character of George
Orwell is kind of similar to how we

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.100
today curate our online identity,
particularly in social media.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.620
Like most people of his generation,
Orwell was fascinated by technology

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.400
and what technology could do,
without ever having any clear idea

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.200
of what it actually practically
consisted of.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.940
Just after the Second World War,
for example, he was given, or bought,

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.120
one of the very first biros,
which had just been invented.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.560
And Orwell thought, I think,

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.420
that this was a kind
of miraculous invention.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.780
Orwell's novel 1984 is full of
technology, and what technology

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:27.120
can do to help oppressive systems
work their evil, basically.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.880
The principle controlling device, the
telescreen, has this extraordinary

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.380
ability to monitor people as they
go about their daily lives,

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.880
and literally spy on almost
everything they do.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.800
But there were also a few spaces
where these telescreens don't exist -

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.540
in working class areas of London,
for example, in the parole pub.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.640
If only that were true in the
contemporary world.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.220
There's no real escaping from
our interactions with screens

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.300
on a daily basis - it's almost
impossible not to look at one.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.200
One thing that's different about
Orwell's most famous book, 1984,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.460
and the present, is that in 1984
the state controls all the media

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.880
and all the messaging that
citizens interact with.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.100
In the present day, there's a much
more diverse landscape in terms of

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.260
how things are reported, and the fact
that we can all, with smartphones

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.020
in our pockets, type about
what's going on in front of us

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.420
and post videos online, is an
important distinction to be made.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:23.460
This said, there are states who try
to suppress people talking about

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.480
things online, and we see this
all over the world.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.400
Some recent examples might be in
Kashmir, where there's been a media

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.820
blackout, and in Xinjiang, in China.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.280
If he were alive today, it's perfectly
conceivable that Orwell

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.640
would have been sifting through
social media, with the idea of

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.440
writing some kind of structural
analysis of how it worked,

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.100
in much the same way that 70, 80 years
before, he would write essays about

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.120
the kind of magazines that teenage
boys read in their spare time.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.660
Orwell, in a way is like the curation
of our digital selves today.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.280
Part of the character of George
Orwell that he creates is to be

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.420
a truth teller -
to tell it down the line.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.840
To tell it like it is.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.320
Orwell may have been troubled
by the way in which mistruths

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.300
and lies and even innocent
mistakes can spread so quickly

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.820
on social media, out of control.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.600
Technology enables this.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:16.500
It's very easy with a smartphone,
or with Twitter and social media

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.640
to sow a seed of disquiet -
to seed an untruth

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.460
into people's consciousness.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.760
Orwell was obsessed with the idea
of future worlds, the shape of things

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.500
to come, and obviously the part that
technology would play in enabling

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.300
those worlds to exist and function.

